    "scope": "8.d.W adds query_records and an owned QueryPage with exactly items/total/offset/limit/returned/truncated. All supplied-inventory filters execute before exact counting, full scalar sorting and pagination. Missing/null/Boolean/numeric/string precedence, NFC UTF-8 string order, mixed directions and final ascending ID tie-breaking have independent result oracles. Projection never removes sort operands. Object/array sort values fail before pagination, and computed verification sorting requires the actual provider even for empty input. Source bytes, query/input bindings and all existing selection behavior remain unchanged. The synchronous kernel is in-memory; R/C continuation and M6 spooling, public CLI/live-project delivery and current semantic providers remain open. 8.d.R adds QueryWork with actual M6 bounded-run sorting and forward-only page construction. Single and multiple advance calls retain exact empty/beyond-end/zero-limit pages, all scalar directions and final ascending ID ties. An actual 10001-item selection is delivered without a count cap. Projection pointer order, original Unicode and input/query bindings survive external merges. Incomplete work never exposes a complete page; cancellation, native write failures and run tampering preserve owned files and block success. Only an explicit private spool outside protected roots is writable. Upstream selection and the requested page remain materialized; this is process-local continuation, not durable Work or a whole-query memory bound. All prior tests remain unchanged. 8.d.C extracts the identical private query row codec, direction comparison, row producer and M6 spool specialization into _paging_spool.py. QueryWork keeps its public module identity, preparation, real advance/delivery orchestration and failure/handle lifecycle. An AST/source proof binds all five moved definitions and the remaining module to the authoritative R Result, allowing only the explicit import changes. All 271 prior test files and 9014 test IDs remain unchanged; results, projection order, original bytes/bindings, scalar ordering, exact counts, errors and allowed storage effects are preserved."
